@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "IN, BETWEEN & IS NULL";
}

<section>

    <!-- PAGE INTRODUCTION -->

    <div class="mb-5">

        <h1 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            IN, BETWEEN &amp; IS NULL
        </h1>

        <p class="lead text-muted">
            IN, BETWEEN and IS NULL are commonly used SQL operators
            for filtering and checking data in SQL Server.
        </p>

        <p>
            These operators make it easier to write conditions when
            working with multiple values, ranges and NULL values.
        </p>

    </div>


    <!-- IN OPERATOR -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            IN Operator
        </h2>

        <p>
            The <strong>IN</strong> operator is used to check whether
            a value matches any value from a specified list.
        </p>

        <p>
            It is useful when we want to check multiple possible
            values without writing several OR conditions.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- IN SYNTAX -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            IN Syntax
        </h2>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>SELECT column_name
FROM table_name
WHERE column_name IN (value1, value2, value3);</code></pre>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- SAMPLE TABLE -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Example Table
        </h2>

        <div class="table-responsive">

            <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                <thead class="table-dark">

                    <tr>
                        <th>StudentId</th>
                        <th>StudentName</th>
                        <th>Age</th>
                        <th>City</th>
                        <th>Fees</th>
                    </tr>

                </thead>

                <tbody>

                    <tr>
                        <td>101</td>
                        <td>Rahul</td>
                        <td>22</td>
                        <td>Pune</td>
                        <td>35000</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>102</td>
                        <td>Priya</td>
                        <td>23</td>
                        <td>Mumbai</td>
                        <td>40000</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>103</td>
                        <td>Amit</td>
                        <td>21</td>
                        <td>Nashik</td>
                        <td>30000</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>104</td>
                        <td>Neha</td>
                        <td>24</td>
                        <td>Pune</td>
                        <td>45000</td>
                    </tr>

                </tbody>

            </table>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- IN EXAMPLE -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Example – IN
        </h2>

        <p>
            Suppose we want students from Pune, Mumbai or Nashik.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>SELECT *
FROM Students
WHERE City IN ('Pune', 'Mumbai', 'Nashik');</code></pre>

        </div>

        <p class="mt-3">
            This returns records where City matches any of the
            specified values.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- IN VS OR -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            IN vs OR
        </h2>

        <p>
            The following two queries can be used to check the same
            set of city values.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>SELECT *
FROM Students
WHERE City = 'Pune'
   OR City = 'Mumbai'
   OR City = 'Nashik';</code></pre>

        </div>

        <p class="mt-3">
            Using IN can make this type of condition shorter and
            easier to read.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- NOT IN -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            NOT IN
        </h2>

        <p>
            The <strong>NOT IN</strong> operator returns records
            whose value does not match any value in the specified
            list.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>SELECT *
FROM Students
WHERE City NOT IN ('Pune', 'Mumbai');</code></pre>

        </div>

        <p class="mt-3">
            This excludes students from Pune and Mumbai.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- BETWEEN -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            BETWEEN Operator
        </h2>

        <p>
            The <strong>BETWEEN</strong> operator is used to select
            values within a specified range.
        </p>

        <p>
            BETWEEN includes both the starting and ending values.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- BETWEEN SYNTAX -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            BETWEEN Syntax
        </h2>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>SELECT column_name
FROM table_name
WHERE column_name BETWEEN value1 AND value2;</code></pre>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- BETWEEN NUMERIC -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            BETWEEN with Numbers
        </h2>

        <p>
            Suppose we want students whose age is between 21 and 23.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>SELECT *
FROM Students
WHERE Age BETWEEN 21 AND 23;</code></pre>

        </div>

        <p class="mt-3">
            This includes students with ages 21, 22 and 23.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- BETWEEN FEES -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            BETWEEN with Fees
        </h2>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>SELECT *
FROM Students
WHERE Fees BETWEEN 30000 AND 40000;</code></pre>

        </div>

        <p class="mt-3">
            This returns students whose fees are between 30000 and
            40000, including both boundary values.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- NOT BETWEEN -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            NOT BETWEEN
        </h2>

        <p>
            The <strong>NOT BETWEEN</strong> operator returns values
            outside the specified range.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>SELECT *
FROM Students
WHERE Age NOT BETWEEN 21 AND 23;</code></pre>

        </div>

        <p class="mt-3">
            This returns students whose age is outside the range
            21 to 23.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- IS NULL -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            IS NULL
        </h2>

        <p>
            <strong>NULL</strong> represents a missing, unknown or
            unavailable value.
        </p>

        <p>
            To check whether a column contains NULL, we use
            <strong>IS NULL</strong>.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>SELECT *
FROM Students
WHERE City IS NULL;</code></pre>

        </div>

        <p class="mt-3">
            This returns records where City does not contain a value.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- IS NOT NULL -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            IS NOT NULL
        </h2>

        <p>
            The <strong>IS NOT NULL</strong> condition returns
            records where a column contains a value.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>SELECT *
FROM Students
WHERE City IS NOT NULL;</code></pre>

        </div>

        <p class="mt-3">
            This returns records where City has a value.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- NULL IMPORTANT -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <div class="alert alert-warning border-0">

            <h4 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Important About NULL
            </h4>

            <ul class="mb-0">

                <li class="mb-2">
                    NULL means the value is missing or unknown.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    NULL is not the same as zero.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    NULL is not the same as an empty string.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Use IS NULL to check for NULL values.
                </li>

                <li>
                    Use IS NOT NULL to check for non-NULL values.
                </li>

            </ul>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- COMBINING OPERATORS -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Combining IN and BETWEEN
        </h2>

        <p>
            IN and BETWEEN can be combined with other conditions
            using AND or OR.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>SELECT *
FROM Students
WHERE City IN ('Pune', 'Mumbai')
AND Age BETWEEN 21 AND 24;</code></pre>

        </div>

        <p class="mt-3">
            This returns students from Pune or Mumbai whose age is
            between 21 and 24.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- COMPARISON TABLE -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            IN vs BETWEEN vs IS NULL
        </h2>

        <div class="table-responsive">

            <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                <thead class="table-dark">

                    <tr>
                        <th>Operator</th>
                        <th>Purpose</th>
                        <th>Example</th>
                    </tr>

                </thead>

                <tbody>

                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>IN</strong></td>
                        <td>Checks multiple specific values.</td>
                        <td>City IN ('Pune', 'Mumbai')</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>BETWEEN</strong></td>
                        <td>Checks values within a range.</td>
                        <td>Age BETWEEN 21 AND 24</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>IS NULL</strong></td>
                        <td>Checks for missing NULL values.</td>
                        <td>City IS NULL</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>IS NOT NULL</strong></td>
                        <td>Checks for values that are not NULL.</td>
                        <td>City IS NOT NULL</td>
                    </tr>

                </tbody>

            </table>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- IMPORTANT POINTS -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <div class="alert alert-info border-0">

            <h4 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Important Points
            </h4>

            <ul class="mb-0">

                <li class="mb-2">
                    IN checks a value against multiple values.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    NOT IN excludes specified values.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    BETWEEN checks a range and includes both boundary
                    values.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    NOT BETWEEN checks values outside a range.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    IS NULL checks for NULL values.
                </li>

                <li>
                    IS NOT NULL checks for non-NULL values.
                </li>

            </ul>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- SUMMARY -->

    <section class="mb-4">

        <div class="alert alert-primary border-0">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Summary
            </h2>

            <p class="mb-0">
                In this section, we learned the IN, NOT IN, BETWEEN,
                NOT BETWEEN, IS NULL and IS NOT NULL operators.
                These operators are useful for filtering SQL Server
                data using multiple values, ranges and NULL checks.
            </p>

        </div>

    </section>


